  <h2>Abstract</h2>
  <p itemprop="description">This article offers a dialogical examination of 1 Kings 17–19 that deconstructs traditionally accepted characterizations of Jezebel, Elijah, and the Widow of Zarephath. The study proposes that shifting emphasis from Elijah and Jezebel as polar opposites to a literary ménage à trois involving all three characters makes it possible for the holy man to become a stranger and the strange women to become holy. The narrative tensions revealed question the validity of Israel’s definitive identity markers.</p>
